<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Contracts only.

    This project must never gain a PackageReference or a ProjectReference.
    It is the single assembly an external production references in order to write
    its own rules, and every dependency added here becomes a dependency every
    plugin author inherits. A test asserts this rather than trusting it.

    The three folders are the three questions a plugin author asks in order:
    Rules holds what they write and what the tool hands back, Services holds
    the interfaces the tool implements for them, and Model holds the data
    those two exchange. The namespaces follow the folders.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Description>Contracts for writing Preflight validation rules.</Description>

    <!--
      Declared here rather than inherited, even though it currently equals the
      product version in Directory.Build.props. This number is the contract
      between the tool and every plugin, and it moves when the surface a
      plugin compiles against moves — which is not the same event as a release
      of the tool. Keeping the declaration local is what lets the two diverge
      the day they need to, without a reader having to work out which of the
      two a given number meant.

      0.3.0, and the leading zero is doing work: while the major is 0, SemVer
      makes the minor the breaking axis, so a plugin built against 0.3.x runs
      on a 0.3.y host and is refused on 0.2.x. The loader and the cache key
      both get that rule from AbstractionsCompatibility.GenerationOf rather
      than reimplementing it.

      The move from 0.2.0 is a minor and not a patch even though the change is
      purely additive — a new interface for reading the process environment,
      and one optional property carrying it. What decides compatibility is the
      generation, and the generation of 0.2.0 is 0.2: a plugin compiled against
      the new surface and published as 0.2.1 would load on a 0.2.0 host that
      has neither, and fail later as a type load far from the cause. Raising
      the minor refuses it early, in a message that names both versions. The
      move from 0.1.1 to 0.2.0 was a minor for exactly the same reason.

      Nothing is published under a stability promise yet. The grouping of the
      contract into Rules, Services and Model would have cost a major on a 1.x
      line — a plugin references its types by full name in metadata, so moving
      them is the same break as deleting them — and on a 0.x line it costs a
      minor instead. The break is identical; what changes is the promise that
      was never made.

      AssemblyVersion is deliberately left to follow Version rather than being
      pinned to <major>.0.0.0, which is a common habit and would collapse the
      first two rows of the compatibility table into "always loads". Guarded by
      AbstractionsDependencyTests.
    -->
    <Version>0.3.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
